        "cap": "Common determinatives (Gardiner’s sign list)."
      },
      {
        "t": "viz",
        "name": "eg-glyphs",
        "opts": {
          "word": "khasut",
          "breakdown": true
        },
        "cap": "The three hills alone: ḫꜣst “foreign land”, the desert hills that rise beyond the flat green valley."
      },
      {
        "t": "h",
        "text": "Two look-alike pairs"
      },
      {
        "t": "p",
        "html": "The three hills <span class=\"eg-h\" style=\"font-size:1.3em;line-height:1\">𓈉</span> mark foreign lands and the desert, which Egyptians grouped together: hilly country outside the flat valley. The mountain <span class=\"eg-h\" style=\"font-size:1.3em;line-height:1\">𓈋</span>, with two peaks and a valley between, is a different sign, the biliteral <i class=\"eg-tl\">ḏw</i> “mountain”. And the papyrus roll <span class=\"eg-h\" style=\"font-size:1.3em;line-height:1\">𓏛</span> is never a sound sign: it marks words for writing (a book, a document) and for abstract ideas such as <i class=\"eg-tl\">mꜣꜥt</i> “truth”."
      },
      {
        "t": "why",
        "html": "Why does an idea like “truth” take a papyrus roll? Because the roll stood for writing and knowledge in general, and from there for anything that cannot be pictured. The choice of determinatives shows how Egyptians grouped the world: buildings with the house, liquids with water, emotions with the mouth."
      },
      {
        "t": "mistake",
        "html": "Confusing the papyrus roll with a sound sign, or the three hills (foreign land) with the mountain sign ḏw. Neither the roll nor the hills is read; the mountain is read ḏw. Check: if a word ends in three hills, think of a foreign land or the desert."
      },
      {
        "t": "ex",
        "title": "Predict the determinative",
        "q": "Which determinative would you expect for <i class=\"eg-tl\">šm</i> “go”?",
        "steps": [
          "It is a verb of motion."
        ],
